Let $W$ in <<ref:als0>> be the Weyr canonical form of the Jordan matrix <<ref:dem0>>. Then the set of matrices that commute with $W$ consists of all matrices of the form

\begin{equation}
S=\left[\begin{MAT}(r){c1c1c1c}
\un S_{11}&\un S_{12}&\ddots &\un S_{1k}\\1
&\un S_{22}&\ \ddots\ &\ddots\\1
&&\ddots&\un S_{\,k-1,k}\\1
0&&&\un S_{\,kk}
\\\end{MAT}\right],\qquad
\un S_{\,\alpha \beta }=
\mat{S_{11}^{(\beta -\alpha)}&\dots&S_{1m_{\beta }}^{(\beta -\alpha) }
\\ \hdotsfor{3}\\
S_{m_{\alpha }1}^{(\beta -\alpha)}&\dots&S_{m_{\alpha }m_{\beta }}^{(\beta -\alpha) }}
\end{equation}

($m_1,m_2,\dots$ are defined in <<ref:dds0>>), in which every $S_{ij}^{(\gamma )}$ is a $p_i\times p_j$ matrix,

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
S_{ij}^{(\gamma) }\text{ is arbitrary}&\text{ \ if \ }
k_j-k_i\le\gamma,
\\
S_{ij}^{(\gamma) }=0&\text{ \ if \ }
k_j-k_i>\gamma.
\end{array}
\end{equation}
